  "image_name": "0d2e0fde25aa9dca9e3a92c465d08b84.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three photocatalytic schemes for splitting water into oxygen and hydrogen, organized around semiconductor band positions relative to the water redox potentials. In the simplest “one-step” design, a single semiconductor absorbs light, creating an electron in the conduction band and a hole in the valence band. These charges migrate to reduction and oxidation cocatalysts, where the electron reduces protons to H2 and the hole oxidizes water to O2. The diagram emphasizes both useful forward charge-transfer paths and possible backward recombination reactions.\n\nThe lower two schemes show “two-step” excitation, where separate light absorbers divide the two half-reactions. One particle functions as an oxygen-evolving photocatalyst (OEP), while the other is a hydrogen-evolving photocatalyst (HEP). In one version, the two are linked by an aqueous redox mediator that shuttles electrons between oxidized and reduced forms. In the other, the connection is a solid-state electron mediator bridging the two semiconductors directly. Overall, the figure illustrates how separating oxidation and reduction into two photoexcited components can better match energy levels for water splitting, while also introducing new interfaces where reverse electron transfer can occur.",
